## 2️⃣ Schéma Relationnel de la Base de Données  

**Entités / Tables** (PK = Primary Key, FK = Foreign Key)  

| Table | Colonnes principales | PK | FK | Relation |
|-------|----------------------|----|----|----------|
| `users` | `id`, `email`, `password_hash`, `created_at` | `id` | – | 1‑N → `bank_accounts` |
| `bank_accounts` | `id`, `user_id`, `provider`, `external_id`, `access_token`, `created_at` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id` | 1‑N → `transactions` |
| `transactions` | `id`, `account_id`, `date`, `amount`, `description`, `category_id`, `is_imported`, `created_at` | `id` | `account_id` → `bank_accounts.id`<br>`category_id` → `categories.id` | N‑1 ← `categories` |
| `categories` | `id`, `name`, `parent_id`, `is_user_defined` | `id` | `parent_id` → `categories.id` (self‑ref) | 1‑N → `transactions` |
| `budgets` | `id`, `user_id`, `category_id`, `period_start`, `period_end`, `limit_amount` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id`<br>`category_id` → `categories.id` | 1‑N → `alerts` |
| `alerts` | `id`, `user_id`, `budget_id`, `triggered_at`, `type` (over‑budget, upcoming) | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id`<br>`budget_id` → `budgets.id` | – |
| `recommendations` | `id`, `user_id`, `title`, `description`, `created_at` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id` | – |
| `ai_jobs` | `id`, `user_id`, `status` (pending/running/done), `started_at`, `finished_at` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id` | – |
| `sessions` | `id`, `user_id`, `refresh_token_hash`, `expires_at` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id` | – |
| `audit_logs` | `id`, `user_id`, `action`, `entity`, `entity_id`, `timestamp` | `id` | `user_id` → `users.id` | – |

**Relations clés**  
- `users` 1‑N `bank_accounts`  
- `bank_accounts` 1‑N `transactions`  
- `categories` 1‑N `transactions` (catégorie de chaque transaction)  
- `categories` auto‑référence (hiérarchie)  
- `users` 1‑N `budgets` → `categories`  
- `budgets` 1‑N `alerts`  
- `users` 1‑N `recommendations`  
- `users` 1‑N `ai_jobs`  
- `users` 1‑N `sessions`  
- `users` 1‑N `audit_logs`  

